13:44:30 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a real logical concern worth answering precisely, because it would be a genuine problem if true — but it rests on collapsing a distinction I've been trying to keep clear the whole conversation. **Nobody's saying you can't state true things about groups.** Here's what's actually fine to say, and I'd say it about any group: - "Country X has a documented drug use rate of Y%, according to [source]." Fine, factual, sayable. - "Region A has higher rates of Z than region B, per this study." Fine, sayable. - "Poverty and conflict are risk factors for substance use in general, across populations." Fine, sayable, well-supported. **What doesn't hold up isn't "saying true things about groups" — it's specific moves that happen to be logically invalid regardless of which group they're applied to:** - Generalizing from a tiny personal sample to an entire continent's population ("every African person I've known...") - Treating "common knowledge" or "what my family always said" as evidence - Taking a real country-level statistic and using it to prejudge one specific individual you haven't met - Collapsing 54 distinct countries into one undifferentiated category None of those four moves become valid when applied to a different group, and none of them become invalid specifically because they're applied to African people. If someone said "every Irish person I've known drinks too much, so the new Irish guy next door is probably a problem," I'd raise the exact same objections, for the exact same reasons. That's the actual test of whether a standard is principled: does it apply the same way regardless of which group is being discussed? This one does. So the answer to "can anyone ever say anything true about a group" is: yes, all the time, with real data, held with appropriate uncertainty, not used to prejudge individuals. What's off the table isn't truth-telling about groups — it's specific reasoning errors that were present in this conversation and would be equally wrong pointed at any other group. The line was never "you can't discuss group patterns." It was "the specific chain of reasoning used to justify suspicion toward one specific person doesn't hold up, and swapping in a different group wouldn't fix it."
