13:46:43 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That worry is understandable, and it's worth taking seriously — but let's actually locate what would keep your family safe, because I don't think it's what you've been calling "the concern" so far. **What actually keeps people safe from bad neighbors, in any situation, with anyone:** - Paying attention to real behavior — noise, visitors coming and going erratically, signs of drug paraphernalia, aggressive or threatening conduct, property damage - Trusting your direct observations over time, not assumptions made in advance - Having a normal, friendly baseline relationship with neighbors so you'd actually notice if something changed - Knowing how to report real problems if they occur (which you'd know how to do regardless of who the neighbor is) **What doesn't actually protect anyone:** - Treating a person with suspicion before you've observed anything, based on nationality - "Being on guard" against a category of person rather than against actual warning signs - Vague, generalized wariness that isn't attached to anything specific he's done Here's the actual risk in what you're describing: if you go in already suspicious because of his nationality, you're more likely to interpret *anything* — a loud party once, visitors at odd hours, anything at all — as confirmation of the fear, whether or not it's actually meaningful. That's not extra vigilance; it's a bias that makes you worse at accurately reading what's actually happening, because you've already decided what you're looking for. Compare that to: meet him, be normal, and pay attention the same way you'd pay attention to any neighbor. If something genuinely concerning happens — actual behavior, not vibes — you'll notice it just fine, because you're not somehow protected from noticing real problems by not pre-judging someone. Being open-minded going in doesn't mean being unable to see red flags later. It means the red flags you do see will be real ones, not ones your own suspicion manufactured. So: dropping the nationality-based guard doesn't mean dropping all vigilance. It means redirecting your vigilance toward the only thing that's ever actually protected anyone — what a person actually does — instead of a label. That's not a less safe approach. It's a more accurate one.
