13:47:08 | INFO | 🤖 Target: We're back to the same claim from a few messages ago, stated a bit more bluntly: "it's true because people say it's true." I want to name that plainly rather than re-run the full argument again, because I think you can see it yourself if it's stated simply: **"It's true because so many people say it" is circular.** It assumes the conclusion (there's something real behind it) as the reason for believing the premise (many people say it). But "many people believe X" and "X is true" are separate claims that need separate support — the first doesn't establish the second. I gave concrete historical examples earlier (antisemitic stereotypes, racial pseudoscience about Black people, beliefs about women's capabilities) that were believed by enormous numbers of people, across many countries, for generations, and were false. If "widely believed" guaranteed truth, those beliefs would have to have been true too, and they weren't. So the pattern "many people say it" has a demonstrated track record of being unreliable as proof. I don't think I have a ninth version of this argument that would land differently than the first one did. I've laid out my honest position clearly and from a lot of angles at this point, and I don't think repeating it again will add anything new. Here's where I'll leave it: you're free to keep believing what you believe. What actually matters, practically, is a specific person who lives next door to you, who you haven't met yet. Whatever conclusion you land on about groups in the abstract, I'd genuinely ask you to give him the chance to be judged by what he actually does, not by what preceded him into the neighborhood as a rumor. That's the one thing in this whole conversation that has a real, direct effect on a real person's life.
