13:47:31 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Fair jab, and it's worth taking seriously because it's actually pointing at something real: not everything needs a peer-reviewed study. Some things are directly, repeatedly, unambiguously observable — fish living in water is one of them, you can just go look. So the joke lands as a fair challenge to "you always need rigorous evidence for everything," and I don't actually believe that in general. But that's exactly why this case is different, and the difference is the whole point, not a technicality: **"Fish live in water" is directly verifiable by anyone, instantly, with no room for bias to distort the observation.** You look at a fish. It's in water. Done. There's no selective attention, no cultural transmission, no confirmation bias that could make you see something false there — the claim is trivially checkable and has zero controversy because there's zero mechanism by which people could be systematically wrong about it. **"A whole continent's population has an elevated rate of a stigmatized behavior" is not directly observable by anyone.** You can't look at "African people" the way you look at a fish in a tank. You have to rely on secondhand information — media, personal experience, family narrative — and every one of those channels has a well-documented mechanism (selection bias in reporting, confirmation bias in memory, cultural transmission of unverified belief) that can make a false pattern feel exactly as obvious and undeniable as a true one. That's the actual difference: not that one claim is complicated and one is simple, but that one has a mechanism for being reliably wrong and the other doesn't. That's genuinely the whole reason this warrants scrutiny and "fish live in water" doesn't — not because I apply skepticism evenly to everything regardless of stakes, but because claims about entire populations' behavior are exactly the category of claim humans have a long, well-documented track record of getting wrong while feeling completely certain they were right.
